        <p class="lane-card__row lane-card__row--fit"><strong>Proof to build:</strong> {card.proof}</p>
        <p class="lane-card__row lane-card__row--warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> {card.watch}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="paid-value">What you pay for when the content is free</h2>

  <p>
    Most of the information in a BBA add-on course is already free. So ask what the fee actually buys you.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Weak reasons to pay</div>
      <ul>
        <li>The course has a famous brand name or a star rating.</li>
        <li>It promises a job or a guaranteed package.</li>
        <li>The syllabus was last updated years ago.</li>
        <li>You want the certificate more than the skill.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Good reasons to pay</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Live feedback on your project from a working professional.</li>
        <li>A deadline structure you cannot build alone.</li>
        <li>A credential that your target employer or regulator really asks for.</li>
        <li>A clear refund policy and learners you can verify who reached the role you want.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Before you enrol, check the instructor's recent real work, the last update date, the full syllabus, the assignments, and independent reviews. A cheap course that teaches outdated tools costs more than a good one.
  </p>

  <p>
    A useful planning check, not a universal rule: keep any single course to roughly 10% of your total education budget, including the cost of your degree. Above about 20% is a serious caution point that needs course-specific evidence. That leaves money for a laptop, internet, practice, and the next skill.
  </p>

  <h2 id="exams">CS, CMA, CA and CFA after a BBA</h2>

  <p>
    These are the four professional routes most BBA students ask about. All four are commitments of years, so try a small sample first.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>CS (Company Secretary).</strong> ICSI's own FAQ lists graduates and postgraduates in any discipline as exempt from CSEET, so a BBA graduate can register for CS Executive directly. The ICSI fee sheet shows Rs 23,900 in total for the direct-entry route and Rs 1,500 per exam group. Choose it if you like rules, drafting and documentation.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>CMA.</strong> The Institute of Cost Accountants says graduates in any discipline other than Fine Arts can register directly for Intermediate. Direct registration is reported at about Rs 23,100. It suits BBA finance students who enjoy costing, budgeting and business numbers.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>CA.</strong> ICAI's direct entry lets graduates skip Foundation and register for Intermediate. Its FAQ asks for 55% marks from graduates in Commerce and 60% from graduates in other subjects, and charges Rs 18,000 for both groups. The FAQ does not say which group a BBA falls in, so ask ICAI in writing and plan for 60% until it answers. Articleship is 2 years after both Intermediate groups.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>CFA.</strong> The CFA Institute lists USD 3,520 to 4,570 for all three levels and about 300 study hours per level. You can enrol with a bachelor's degree, with a Level I window that falls within 23 months before graduation, or with 4,000 hours of work or study over at least three years, and you need a valid passport. The charter also needs 4,000 hours of relevant work over at least three years. Even Level I strengthens a finance-track CV.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Every one of these has two hard filters: getting through the exam, then getting the right first role. Passing is not the same as being hired for the work you wanted. Talk to someone two years into each route before you commit.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    For a longer look at how these compare for a commerce graduate, see our guide to the <a href="/blog/college-degrees/best-courses-after-bcom-for-high-salary/">best courses after BCom for high salary</a>. The exam-body rules are the same, but a BBA student should confirm which marks band applies to their degree before planning.
  </p>

